9. Example 

Code:

class student:
      def __init__(self, name):
              self.name = name
s1 = student("NamanNirwal")
print(s1.name)

Output of the above code:

NamanNirwal

What is happening in the above Code ?
Line 1: Creating  a class named "Student". Think of it as a blueprint for creating student objects.
Line 2 to 3: the __init__ method -> this method runs automatically when you create a new object. 
Its accepts two parameters.
1. Self -> refers to the object being created.
2. name -> receives the name provide you when creating the object.
Line 3: Storing the name 
self.name = name 
This is the most important line.
self.name is the name attribute stored in the object
name is the value received by the method 
when we write student("NamanNirwal") ---> the value "NamanNirwal" passed to the name.
Therefore, python stores "NamanNirwal" in self.name. 
Line 4: Creating the object
s1 = student("NamanNirwal")
here are three important things happen :
1. Python creates a new object S1.
2. Python automatically calls __init__ method and passes "NamanNirwal" as the "name" argument.
3. self.name stores that value and object is assigned to s1. 
Line 5: Printing the name.
Python accesses the name attribute of object s1 and prints its value.






